1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,175
Could lightning
power the world?

2
00:00:02,175 --> 00:00:04,750
Catch every bolt,
for a whole year.

3
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:09,045
Lightning is flashing
somewhere right now,

4
00:00:09,045 --> 00:00:12,035
about 44 times
every second,

5
00:00:12,145 --> 00:00:13,695
all around the planet.

6
00:00:13,835 --> 00:00:15,300
That adds up to more

7
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,760
than 1 billion
flashes in a year.

8
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:19,700
One flash carries

9
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:21,695
roughly
1 billion joules,

10
00:00:21,825 --> 00:00:24,240
a rough guess
with a wide range.

11
00:00:24,465 --> 00:00:27,520
Multiply it by every
flash in the year.

12
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,790
That is about
400 billion

13
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:31,540
kilowatt-hours.

14
00:00:32,355 --> 00:00:34,515
But a number only means

15
00:00:34,515 --> 00:00:36,025
something
beside another,

16
00:00:36,055 --> 00:00:38,885
so let's compare
it with what we use.

17
00:00:39,785 --> 00:00:42,560
The world uses
nearly 80 times

18
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,005
more electricity
in a year.

19
00:00:45,005 --> 00:00:46,745
All the lightning
on Earth

20
00:00:46,745 --> 00:00:49,140
would run
it for about five days.

21
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,895
If a flash holds
10 times less

22
00:00:52,895 --> 00:00:55,425
or 10 times more,
it swings

23
00:00:55,425 --> 00:00:58,175
from half a day
to about seven weeks.

24
00:00:59,005 --> 00:01:01,670
Catching
it is the real problem.

25
00:01:01,710 --> 00:01:05,605
Most of a flash becomes
heat, light and sound,

26
00:01:05,605 --> 00:01:08,540
and each bolt is over
in a split second.

27
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:14,519
If your brain's doing the math with me, tap like and follow.
